        <section>
            <h2>Famous Commonplacers</h2>

            <h3>John Locke</h3>
                <p>John Locke (1632-1704) was an influential English philosopher, and
                physician during the Enlightenment movement. Known for his foundational
                work in epistemology and political philosophy. He is also remembered
                for his approach to commonplace books. In 1685, he developed an indexing
                method in his essay titled A New Method of Making Common-Place Books. Locke's commonplace 
                book was an organized, indexed repository that was designed to keep records of excerpts, reflections, 
                and citations without forcing entries into rigid categories or from having the dilemma of a
                disorganized notebook.</p>

            <h3>Thomas Jefferson</h3>
                <p>In the 18th Century, the 3rd President of the United States Thomas
                Jefferson kept both literature and legal based commonplace books.
                He would include topics of interest such as politics, history, law, and philosophy.</p>

